# Real multilingual-first revision (2026-08-07, same day as the 70B swap
# below it replaces): cs-opus targeted meta-llama/Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct
# briefly, then was corrected to CohereLabs/Aya-Expanse-32B once real
# multilingual coverage was confirmed as a hard requirement. Verified via
# real research before this change (not assumed):
#   - Aya-101 (13B, 101 real languages) was the first candidate but is a
#     T5 encoder-decoder architecture -- llama.cpp/Ollama only supports
#     decoder-only (CausalLM) models, confirmed via
#     https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/issues/5763 (open, unresolved).
#     Genuinely cannot run on this platform's serving stack, not just
#     suboptimal.
#   - Aya Expanse 32B is real, decoder-only (Command-family lineage),
#     23 real languages with deep per-language quality (not 101 -- an
#     honest tradeoff, not the number originally asked for), 128K real
#     context, and confirmed directly pullable from Ollama's own library
#     (19 quantized versions listed, incl. Ollama/llama.cpp-compatible).
# `ollama pull aya-expanse:32b` (real, official library reference) then:
#   ollama create cs-opus -f Modelfile.cs-opus
# (this FROM line only resolves once that pull has actually happened on
# whatever host serves CS_INFERENCE_URL — it is not itself the download)
FROM aya-expanse:32b
# CORRECTION (2026-08-08, real production cutover attempt): every comment
# below assuming "the same 40-80GB pod" was wrong -- the REAL pod actually
# serving CS_INFERENCE_URL is a single RTX A5000, 24GB total VRAM, shared
# with cs-sonnet/cs-haiku/cs-embed/vision/Chatterbox TTS all needing to
# coexist. Confirmed the hard way: num_gpu 99 (full offload) genuinely
# OOM'd in production -- real error, not a guess: "cudaMalloc failed: out
# of memory, alloc_tensor_range: failed to allocate CUDA0 buffer of size
# 19789938688" (~18.4GB for the core weight buffer alone, even with every
# other model evicted first). This model's real quantized footprint
# (~18-20GB at Q4_K_M) does NOT fit this real card at full offload with
# any other model resident, and barely fits even alone.
# num_ctx 8192 comfortably covers the ~4700-token shared system prompt
# (middleware/brain.js) with real headroom to spare -- Aya Expanse's real
# max context is 128K, so this is deliberately conservative for response
# latency, not a hardware ceiling. Raise if a real workload needs more --
# but only after re-running training/benchmark_context_window.js against
# the REAL deployed model first (see that script's header for why). Ran
# it 2026-08-07 against the only host actually reachable at the time
# (this local dev machine, CS_INFERENCE_URL=localhost:11434) and it
# honestly reported the OLD 7.2B model is still what's loaded there, not
# the real 32B Aya Expanse target -- no pod is reachable from this
# environment yet to benchmark the real target. That same run's real
# numbers for the OLD model: 8192 measured 3.2 tok/s (47s wall for 44
# tokens), and 16384+ failed outright (real 500s, this machine's actual
# ceiling) -- so even the current value isn't margin to spare on THIS
# hardware. None of that transfers to the real 32B-on-GPU-pod case; it
# only proves the benchmark script itself works end-to-end. Re-run it
# for real once `ollama pull aya-expanse:32b` has actually happened on
# the real inference host, and let ITS numbers pick this value.
# RAISED 2026-08-28, real benchmark (training/benchmark_context_window.js,
# run against this exact deployed pod/model -- 32.3B command-r family,
# confirmed via /api/show, not assumed): tokens/sec stayed FLAT (20.2-20.3)
# across every successful candidate from 8192 up to 65536 -- zero real
# degradation. (num_ctx=4096 itself timed out on the very first, cold-model
# request; not a sign 4096 is somehow worse than the larger sizes that
# immediately followed once warm.) 65536 is the script's own recommendation
# and exactly half Aya Expanse's real 128K ceiling, so real headroom remains
# for a future push. See that script's printed table for the raw numbers.
PARAMETER num_ctx 65536
PARAMETER num_batch 256
# UPDATED (2026-08-23, real GPU upgrade — A5000 24GB -> A40 48GB): the
# partial-offload constraint above was specific to the OLD 24GB card and
# genuinely no longer applies. Real numbers on the new card: nvidia-smi
# reports 46068 MiB usable. Full 40-layer offload needs the same real
# ~18868 MiB (19789938688 bytes, confirmed OOM error above) regardless of
# card. Budgeting cs-sonnet (~2500 MiB per its own Modelfile's "2B fits
# in 4GB" claim), cs-haiku (~1500 MiB), cs-embed + CUDA/OS overhead
# (~1500 MiB) alongside it: 18868 + 2500 + 1500 + 1500 = 24368 MiB total,
# leaving ~21700 MiB of real spare headroom on this card. Full GPU
# offload (99, matching cs-haiku/cs-sonnet's own "all layers" convention)
# is genuinely safe now -- this should also fix the repeated real
# "cs-opus timeout of 60000ms exceeded" failures from the partial-CPU-
# offload path on the old card. If this pod's usage pattern changes
# (more models resident, larger context) re-verify real free VRAM before
# assuming this still holds, same discipline as the original comment.
PARAMETER num_gpu 99
PARAMETER temperature 0.72
PARAMETER num_predict 8192
